  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Lingering spotty rain and snow showers wind down \nfrom west to east this afternoon, though clouds will linger through \ntonight.\n\nLeftover spotty light rain and higher elevation snow showers are now \nfading across western New York...with this trend expected to spread \neastward through early this evening as as ridging over the Upper \nMidwest continues building into our region along with increasing \nsubsidence and drying aloft. While there may be some additional \nshort-lived breaks of partial sunshine...this drying trend will not \nyet be strong enough to scour out the remnant lower clouds...which \nwill continue to hang tough tonight and support low temps a bit \nmilder than blended guidance. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will follow for Monday-Saturday, along \nwith a return to above normal temperatures by Tuesday.\n\nThe axis of expansive ridging at both the surface and aloft will \nbuild into our region on Monday. After a mostly cloudy start... \ncontinued subsidence attendant to the ridge will gradually help to \nscour out lingering lower clouds Monday and Monday night. Otherwise \nairmass modification and the return of at least some partial \nsunshine should allow highs to climb back to the lower-mid 40s \nacross the higher terrain and the mid-upper 40s across the lower \nelevations...readings that will be just a smidge below to right \naround normal for this point in November.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday the surface ridge will settle across the Mid-\nAtlantic States while its parent upper ridge amplifies across the \nOhio and Tennessee Valleys...with both features then temporarily \nflattening/settling a little further southeast Thursday and Friday \nas a mid-level shortwave trough and associated surface low make \ntheir way across central/northern Ontario and Quebec Provinces.\nModel guidance remains in good agreement on keeping this latter \nsystem and any associated precipitation to our north...and if this \nverifies it would allow the aforementioned ridging and dry weather \nto prevail right through the upcoming week and the first half of \nnext weekend. \n\nAs the axis of the surface ridge slips to our southeast Monday night \nand Tuesday...it will allow a southwesterly flow of progressively \nmilder air to overspread our region for the Tuesday-Thursday period. \nThis will translate into a return to notably above normal \ntemperatures by Wednesday and Thursday...with daytime highs on the \nlatter day likely to reach the upper 50s-lower 60s across the lower \nelevations. The passage of a weak and moisture-starved cool front \ntrailing from the above mentioned surface low may then induce some \nbrief/limited cooling for Friday...before building ridging and \ndeveloping southerly flow brings a return to warmer readings on \nSaturday...when many lower elevation locations currently appear \nlikely to reach or surpass the 60 degree mark.",
